<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#hassaniya-arabic">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hassaniya Arabic</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hassaniya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hassaniya Arabic is the variety of Arabic spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sahara">Western Sahara</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>. It descends from the speech of the Beni Hassan Arab tribes who moved into the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and largely replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-languages">Berber languages</a> of the region. It is the everyday language of the Sahrawi people and of most Mauritanians.</p></div>
